It's about time that the charisma stat was given more usefulness. I
have the following idea:

High charisma means that people are more likely to do what you tell
them. Extending this idea to Nethack could be done by introducing a
command that allows you to order monsters to do things. I suggest
the command #yell, which when you use it, asks for a direction, and
then lets you type in a sequence of Nethack actions, such as "kkk"
meaning move up three squares. Monsters which you might have some
influence over (eg tame ones), which are standing in the direction
given, might then do as you say. The probability of obeying and the
length of the command obeyable would be heavily dependant on charisma.

This would contribute greatly to the complexity of combat tactics, if
you now have partial control over monsters other than yourself. You
could tell your dog to block a corridor while you escape from oncoming
hill orcs, for example. I'd also suggest introducing a new spell which
allows you to shout an order that any monster which hears it must obey
(subject to sufficient charisma as usual). So you could for example
tell a troublesome lich to go jump in the moat, or attack something
other than you.

Of course I'm not going to be satisfied until the Wizard of Yendor can
use this spell on you, and the only way to ward it off is to deafen
yourself with an explosion :-)

